===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[official]] who is part of a [[bureaucracy]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Josie Ensor|title=Meet Elon Musk’s ‘baby-faced assassins’ leading Doge takeover|magazine=w:The Times|url=https://www.thetimes.com/us/american-politics/article/who-are-doge-elon-musk-baby-faced-assassins-0tj2qq0mv|date=2025-02-07|passage=Coristine, who had signed onto the telemeeting using a non-government email address, gave the '''bureaucrats''' swift marching orders, telling them he needed access to the federal department’s contract and payment systems before reportedly making them justify their jobs at the $1 billion-budget administration.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Marshall Cohen|title=The almighty Musk: How the world’s richest man became Washington’s most powerful bureaucrat|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/02/14/politics/elon-musk-most-powerful-bureaucrat-washington/index.html|date=2025-02-14|passage=At the Oval Office signing ceremony, Musk responded to critiques that he’s become the very thing he claims to be fighting — an unelected '''bureaucrat''' with enormous power — by pointing to the 2024 results: “You couldn’t ask for a stronger mandate from the public.”}}
# {{lb|en|wiki}} A [[user]] on a [[wiki]] with the right to change user access levels.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|editors=Pnina Fichman; Noriko Hara|title=Global Wikipedia: International and Cross-Cultural Issues in Online Collaboration|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=9780810891029|page=18|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=L0HcDQAAQBAJ&pg=PA18&dq=%22bureaucrat%22+wikipedia&hl=&cd=1&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22bureaucrat%22%20wikipedia&f=false|text=One of three major processes is generally used: a '''bureaucrat''' review, a broader community review, or an approvals group review. As only local '''bureaucrats''' or Wikimedia stewards can grant the bot flag necessary to operate an authorized bot, the simplest method is to appeal directly to these individuals.}}

====Usage notes====
The term {{m|en|bureaucrat}}, while often used in a professional and respectful manner, is oftentimes disdained by those who work in organizations, especially governmental organizations. This is due to connotations of rigidity, indifference, and especially laziness that the term can sometimes connote (the latter especially reflected in the oft-repeated derisive term ''[[lazy]] bureaucrat''). As a result, many workers in organizations, especially governmental ones, prefer terms such as {{m|en|manager}}, {{m|en|public manager}}, {{m|en|civil servant}}, {{m|en|public servant}}, {{m|en|public official}}, etc.

